23. DATA AND BACKEND READINESS

Build the application so it can later connect to a real backend.

Prefer Supabase or Firebase for:

- Authentication.
- Database.
- File storage.
- User profiles.
- Listings.
- Manufacturing requests.
- Offers.
- Notifications.

If a real backend is available and properly configured, integrate it using the appropriate credentials and services.

If no backend is configured, use clearly identified demo data and local persistence for the prototype.

Demo mode requirements:

- Use realistic seed data.
- Make forms actually add records to the local demo state.
- Persist suitable demo records using localStorage or another appropriate browser storage mechanism.
- Ensure searches and filters work with those records.
- Ensure status changes update the interface.
- Keep data access behind reusable service functions so the backend can replace the demo implementation later.

Important limitations:

- Do not describe localStorage as secure authentication.
- Do not claim local browser data is shared with other users.
- Do not claim that a manufacturing request has been delivered to real factories unless a real backend and notification system are connected.
- Do not claim that an offer is a legally binding agreement.
- Do not expose private credentials or API keys in frontend source code.

Make the difference between demo functionality and production integrations clear.

